STEP 1 — Configure the DUT into the topology                            PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Carried over from 38393: po1 = port1.0.1 + port2.0.9 (to AR4050),
po2 = port1.0.9 + port2.0.1 (to x230), both LACP, both spanning the stack.
Changed for this test — split the single bridged vlan 393 into two routed vlans:

  vlan database ; vlan 394 name rt-test-a ; vlan 395 name rt-test-b
                  vlan 394-395 state enable
  interface po1 -> switchport access vlan 394
  interface po2 -> switchport access vlan 395
  interface vlan1   -> no ip address          (was 10.38.215.20/27)
  vlan database -> no vlan 393                (removes old bridged vlan + its IP)
  interface vlan394 -> ip address 10.38.215.20/27
  interface vlan395 -> ip address 10.38.215.34/27

Verified: vlan394 and vlan395 both admin up / running; DUT pings tb470 eth1
(10.38.215.1) over vlan394/po1 and tb470 eth2 (10.38.215.33) over vlan395/po2,
0% loss both.

AR4050 and x230 unchanged from 38393 (each is a plain L2 bridge with its own
2-port LACP LAG; their local vlan IDs 1 and 100 are irrelevant to the DUT since
both DUT ports are untagged access).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 2 — DUT should be routing traffic to 100+ networks                 PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Next-hops resolved by STATIC ARP to FICTITIOUS MACs:

  arp 10.38.215.25 0200.0a00.0001 po1      (A side, vlan394)
  arp 10.38.215.50 0200.0b00.0001 po2      (B side, vlan395)

  show arp:
    10.38.215.25   0200.0a00.0001   vlan394   po1   static
    10.38.215.50   0200.0b00.0001   vlan395   po2   static

WHY FICTITIOUS MACs — this is a deliberate safety measure, not a shortcut.
If the next-hops pointed at tb470's REAL interface MACs, tb470 would receive
packets for 10.200.x/10.201.x, find no route for them, and forward them out its
CORPORATE DEFAULT GATEWAY (10.36.255.254) at 1000+ pps. Pointing the next-hop at
a MAC nobody owns keeps every routed frame captive on the bench, while tcpdump
still sees them because it puts the NIC in promiscuous mode. (Same NIC
hardware-filtering behaviour that produced a false "total packet loss" reading
in the earlier QoS work — here it is used on purpose.)

240 static routes loaded (120 per direction):
  ip route 10.200.<1..120>.0/24 10.38.215.50     -> egress po2
  ip route 10.201.<1..120>.0/24 10.38.215.25     -> egress po1

  awplus# show ip route summary
    Route Source    Networks
    connected       2
    static          240
    Total           242

242 networks — comfortably the "100+" the test asks for. All 240 lines pushed
with ZERO rejected commands.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 3 — Start the traffic. Expected: traffic should forward             PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Two pcaps (600 packets each = 120 networks x 5 hosts), addressed at L2 to the
DUT's VMAC so the DUT must ROUTE them:

  side A (out eth1): Ether(00f0.4d00.7716 -> 0000.cd37.0d6f)
                     IP(10.38.215.1  -> 10.200.<n>.<h>) ttl 64
  side B (out eth2): Ether(00f0.4d00.7717 -> 0000.cd37.0d6f)
                     IP(10.38.215.33 -> 10.201.<n>.<h>) ttl 64

tcpreplay --pps=3000 --loop=3 => 1800 frames sent per side.
Counted at the far end by the next-hop destination MAC:

  arriving at eth2 (eth.dst == 02:00:0b:00:00:01) : 1800 frames, 120 /24s, TTL 63
  arriving at eth1 (eth.dst == 02:00:0a:00:00:01) : 1800 frames, 120 /24s, TTL 63

1800 sent = 1800 received in BOTH directions. ZERO LOSS, and every one of the
120 destination networks per direction is represented.

TTL 63 (down from 64) on every received frame is the key evidence: the frames
were ROUTED by the DUT, not bridged. The destination MAC being the static-ARP
next-hop confirms the egress path as well.

Continuous traffic then started for steps 4-7:
  tcpreplay -i eth1 --pps=1000 --loop=0 rt-a.pcap
  tcpreplay -i eth2 --pps=1000 --loop=0 rt-b.pcap

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 4 — Check the route table. Expected: same across all stack members  PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
(a) Software route table:
      awplus# remote-diff all show ip route
      % Comparing "show ip route" on node 1 and node 2
      % Results are identical

(b) Hardware route table — `show platform table ip`.
    This command prints BOTH members' tables in one output
    ("Stack member 1: [Instance 0]" / "Stack member 2: [Instance 1]") and ships
    its own column legend, which usefully confirms:
      Dev   =  Instance Number in route entry (HW)
      Port  =  Front panel port number in route entry (HW)
      LTT   =  Lookup translation table entry (SW)
      REIdx =  Route entry that prefix points to (SW)
    (That legend independently confirms the reading of `Dev` used in 38393.log —
    it is a per-unit instance index, not forwarding data.)

    Sample rows (member 1):
      IP address/prefLen LTT   REIdx Route-Entry     E Dev Port Tr VID  Mtu   Ttl Cnt
      10.38.215.1     32 2     7     00f0.4d00.7716  0  -   -   127394  1500  Ena 2
      10.38.215.50    32 1     6     0200.0b00.0001  0  -   -   126395  1500  Ena 2
      10.200.1.0      24 4607  6     0200.0b00.0001  0  -   -   126395  1500  Ena 2
    (Tr and VID run together in the raw output: Tr=127/VID=394 for po1/vlan394,
    Tr=126/VID=395 for po2/vlan395.)

    Programmatic comparison of all parsed rows, member 1 vs member 2:
      routes parsed .......................... 247 vs 247
      same prefix set ........................ True
      only in member 1 / only in member 2 .... 0 / 0
      LTT differs on ......................... 0 prefixes
      REIdx differs on ....................... 0 prefixes
      Route-Entry (next-hop MAC) differs on .. 0 prefixes
      E / Dev / Port / Tr,VID / Mtu / Ttl .... 0 prefixes each

    PERFECT MATCH — every field of every route identical on both members.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 5 — Reboot the stack master. Expected: recover within 6 seconds     PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Master at the time was member 1. `reboot stack-member 1` issued from member 1's
own console in privileged mode, with continuous traffic running and tcpdump
capturing both ends.

Outage measured as the largest inter-arrival gap in ROUTED frames received at
the far end (filtered on the static-ARP next-hop destination MAC):

  A->B  eth1 -> AR4050 -> DUT(routed) -> x230 -> eth2
        195787 frames over a 196.7 s window
        largest gap  0.883 s   at epoch 1787092193.743
        next         0.002 s
        next         0.002 s

  B->A  eth2 -> x230 -> DUT(routed) -> AR4050 -> eth1
        207451 frames over a 207.9 s window
        largest gap  0.006 s   at epoch 1787092193.753
        next         0.004 s
        next         0.003 s

WORST-CASE OUTAGE 883 ms vs a 6000 ms expectation — PASS with ~7x margin.
Both directions break at the SAME instant (epoch ...193.74/.75), which is when
member 1's ports dropped. Everything else is sub-10 ms jitter.

The asymmetry is consistent with 38393 (which measured 814 ms / 22 ms): one
direction re-converges essentially instantly while the other takes most of a
second. Here the slow direction is A->B (egress po2); in 38393 it was B->A.
So the ~0.8 s is not tied to a particular LAG or member — it tracks the
failover itself.

Member 1 rebooted FROM FLASH ("Loading flash:IE520-tb470.rel..."), with no
TFTP attempt and no boot loop, confirming the bootloader fix from 38393.log
has held on member 1.

