STEP 6 — Wait for stack to form                                          PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ID  MAC             Priority  Status  Role
  1   84e3.2787.0740  128       Ready   Backup Member
  2   84e3.2787.09c0  128       Ready   Active Master
  Operational Status  Normal operation

Master role SWAPPED to member 2, as expected. Both LAGs restored with all four
legs synchronized, and `show ip route summary` still read 242 routes
(2 connected + 240 static) throughout.

As in 38393, member 1 sat in "Syncing" for several minutes before reaching
Ready, while the data path was ALREADY fully restored — both aggregators showed
all legs synchronized and traffic was flowing during the entire Syncing period.
"Syncing" is not a traffic-affecting state on this platform.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 7 — Check the route table again. Expected: same across all members  PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
(a) awplus# remote-diff all show ip route
    % Comparing "show ip route" on node 2 and node 1
    % Results are identical
    (node order reversed — roles have swapped)

(b) The test case asks for `remote-diff all show platform table ip`.
    THAT COMMAND IS REJECTED ON IE520:
      awplus# remote-diff all show platform table ip
                                  ^
      % Invalid input detected at '^' marker.
    The caret sits under "platform" — remote-diff does not accept the platform
    keyword (the same limitation hit with `show platform table macaddr` in
    38393). It is not needed: `show platform table ip` already prints both
    members' tables in a single output, so they can be compared directly.

(c) show platform table ip, member 1 vs member 2:
      routes parsed .......................... 247 vs 247
      same prefix set ........................ True
      only in member 1 / only in member 2 .... 0 / 0
      test networks present .................. 10.200.x/24 = 120
                                               10.201.x/24 = 120
      REIdx  differs on ...................... 0 prefixes
      Route-Entry (next-hop MAC) differs on .. 0 prefixes
      E / Dev / Port / Tr,VID / Mtu / Ttl .... 0 prefixes each
      LTT    differs on ...................... 4 prefixes   <- see below

    THE 4 LTT DIFFERENCES ARE BENIGN — allocation order, not forwarding data.
    They are exactly the four ARP-derived /32 host routes, and the LTT values
    are the SAME SET on both members ({0,1,2,3}), merely assigned in a different
    order. Every forwarding field on all four is identical:

      prefix            m1 LTT  m2 LTT  REIdx  next-hop MAC     Tr/VID
      10.38.215.1/32    0       2       7      00f0.4d00.7716   127394
      10.38.215.25/32   1       0       5      0200.0a00.0001   127394
      10.38.215.33/32   2       3       8      00f0.4d00.7717   126395
      10.38.215.50/32   3       1       6      0200.0b00.0001   126395
      (REIdx / next-hop MAC / Tr,VID match on all four: True)
      (LTT multiset m1 == LTT multiset m2: True)

    LTT is documented by the command's own legend as "Lookup translation table
    entry (SW)" — a software-side slot index. Member 1 re-learned its four ARP
    entries after rebooting and happened to take the slots in a different order.
    Step 4, before the reboot, showed ZERO LTT differences, which is consistent
    with this being learn-order and nothing else. The forwarding outcome —
    which route points at which next-hop out which trunk — is identical.

(d) Routing re-verified live after the reboot (fresh 12 s capture):
      arriving at eth2 : 12104 routed frames, 120 distinct /24s, TTL 63
      arriving at eth1 : 13528 routed frames, 120 distinct /24s, TTL 63
    Full path working in both directions across all 120 networks per side.
